# ShiftSwap — Business Case

## 1. Value Proposition & Role of IT

ShiftSwap is a web-based shift trading and floor coverage platform built for hourly retail staff and store managers at busy specialty shops, such as our example store, **SlapShot Outfitters** (a hockey and sporting-goods retailer). In most small and mid-sized retail stores, shift changes are still handled through messy group text messages, paper notes left on a breakroom clipboard, or last-minute phone calls to the floor manager. That informal process creates headaches for everyone. When an associate gets sick or has a class conflict, they have to text twenty coworkers individually to find someone willing to take their hours. Meanwhile, floor managers waste hours each week playing middleman, checking whether the replacement worker knows how to handle specialized tasks—like running the skate sharpener—and making sure nobody accidentally crosses into overtime pay.

ShiftSwap solves this problem by giving the store a single, live shift board accessible from any phone or computer. Staff can post an open shift with the exact time, floor station, and skill notes in seconds, and eligible coworkers can claim those extra hours with one tap. Managers get an instant view of which shifts are open, which ones are covered, and how fast gaps are being filled without having to chase people down.

For the revenue model, ShiftSwap uses a simple B2B Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) subscription charged to the retail business rather than the hourly workers. Charging employees to pick up shifts would stop them from using the app, so the store owner pays a predictable monthly fee based on store size:
* **Single-Team Starter Tier (Free):** Up to 10 staff members at one location, designed to let a store test the board with zero upfront risk.
* **Pro Store Tier ($39/month per location):** Unlimited staff, station skill tags, urgent shift alerts, and manager approval controls.
* **Multi-Store Regional Tier ($29/month per location for 5+ stores):** Cross-store shift sharing so nearby branches can borrow staff during weekend tournaments or holiday rushes.

Looking at Porter’s Value Chain, ShiftSwap directly changes two major activities: **Operations** (a primary activity) and **Human Resource Management** (a support activity). In daily store operations, an uncovered shift at the skate sharpening counter or cash desk slows down customer checkout and leads to lost sales during peak hours. By turning shift coverage into a self-serve board, IT replaces manual phone trees with automated matching. In human resource management, IT removes the administrative bottleneck of schedule maintenance and gives managers real data on shift coverage rates and employee reliability. This matters financially because even saving a floor manager four to five hours of scheduling busywork every week pays for the $39 monthly subscription several times over while keeping customer-facing stations fully staffed.

